# XSH crypt()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The algorithm is implementation-defined; only what the page states is checked: the result begins with the
# two salt bytes and lives in static data. That the same key and salt give the same result is not stated and
# is not checked.

assertion  XSH.crypt.001
anchor     functions/crypt.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/crypt.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI+CRYPT
since      1988
says       For each of several valid salts (two bytes, and a longer string) and
           several keys (including an empty and a long one), crypt() returns a
           pointer to a string of at least two bytes whose first two bytes are
           those of the salt.
note       A null pointer with ENOSYS reports NOTINUSE (the page lets the
           function be unsupported); a null pointer with another errno is
           UNRESOLVED. Which characters follow the salt is not examined.
test       t/001-salt.c

assertion  XSH.crypt.002
anchor     functions/crypt.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI+CRYPT
since      1988
says       The string crypt() returns is static data that the next call
           overwrites: after a second call with another key and salt, the pointer
           from the first call reads as the second result.
note       The text says the data is overwritten by each call, so the first
           pointer reads the second result.
test       t/002-static.c
